1
00:00:02,640 --> 00:00:05,080
We are living in an
era of crises

2
00:00:05,080 --> 00:00:08,200
worldwide, around one
billion people are suffering

3
00:00:08,240 --> 00:00:09,920
from stress-related illnesses

4
00:00:09,920 --> 00:00:11,600
and that
number is rising.

5
00:00:14,960 --> 00:00:17,240
The key to resilience
is staying healthy

6
00:00:17,240 --> 00:00:19,960
even when faced with
life’s great burdens.

7
00:00:21,800 --> 00:00:25,320
What keeps people mentally
healthy in spite of serious crises

8
00:00:25,520 --> 00:00:27,200
while others
break down?

9
00:00:30,800 --> 00:00:34,160
Resilience is a natural
phenomenon that enables people

10
00:00:34,240 --> 00:00:37,080
to continue developing
after they experience

11
00:00:37,160 --> 00:00:39,280
a catastrophe or
traumatic event.

12
00:00:42,600 --> 00:00:46,240
Leading researchers are trying
to identify the secret to resilience.

13
00:00:47,960 --> 00:00:51,480
As resilience researchers,
we aim to identify strategies

14
00:00:51,520 --> 00:00:55,440
that can help prevent people
from developing mental illnesses.

15
00:00:57,120 --> 00:00:59,000
Can we learn
to be resilient?

16
00:01:29,320 --> 00:01:32,160
It was as if part of me
had been ripped out.

17
00:01:35,280 --> 00:01:38,520
It’s the greatest loss that
a father can experience.

18
00:01:42,400 --> 00:01:47,160
Georg Ballmann’s son Luca was
killed here on a January evening.

19
00:01:54,640 --> 00:01:59,000
Luca’s mother Helen called me on
a Saturday morning just after seven.

20
00:02:01,400 --> 00:02:05,560
She just said one
thing: “Luca is dead”.

21
00:02:08,640 --> 00:02:12,480
After a birthday party at a
club, Luca and his friend Freddy,

22
00:02:12,520 --> 00:02:15,840
both 16, got into an argument
with another group of teenagers.

23
00:02:17,080 --> 00:02:19,080
They tried to
calm things down.

24
00:02:19,440 --> 00:02:22,520
But the situation escalated
and ended with Freddy

25
00:02:22,560 --> 00:02:24,720
being pushed in front
of an oncoming train.

26
00:02:26,360 --> 00:02:28,360
Luca was pulled
along with him.

27
00:02:36,120 --> 00:02:40,520
It’s a tragedy that Georg Ballmann
shares with Céline and Björn Wilke,

28
00:02:40,600 --> 00:02:41,960
Freddy’s parents.

29
00:02:42,000 --> 00:02:44,280
They all grieve
for their two sons.

30
00:02:46,520 --> 00:02:50,920
In 2019, the police appeared at
the Wilkes’ door early in the morning.

31
00:02:57,880 --> 00:02:59,760
They said we
should sit down.

32
00:03:00,600 --> 00:03:05,640
It was the worst sentence of my
life, really just that one sentence:

33
00:03:05,640 --> 00:03:07,320
Your son
died last night.

34
00:03:12,040 --> 00:03:17,880
And you’re sitting there at the
table, you don’t want to believe it,

35
00:03:17,880 --> 00:03:20,760
but you don’t say “this
can’t be happening”.

36
00:03:20,800 --> 00:03:25,120
It’s as if the earth is opening up
and sucking your whole soul away.

37
00:03:25,160 --> 00:03:27,480
That your whole
world is collapsing.

38
00:03:35,880 --> 00:03:40,480
The day plays out like a bad movie
and you don’t realize what’s happened.

39
00:03:41,400 --> 00:03:44,440
You call a few people and
then you’re sitting at home.

40
00:03:44,680 --> 00:03:48,560
We were lucky that friends came to
us very quickly and looked after us.

41
00:04:00,160 --> 00:04:04,120
I fully understand
people who can’t cope.

42
00:04:04,120 --> 00:04:08,960
I felt that too: why do I get
to live - but my son doesn’t?

43
00:04:16,680 --> 00:04:20,640
The two families have an endlessly
long and painful road ahead.

44
00:04:29,320 --> 00:04:31,920
Stress and even
crises are part of life.

45
00:04:31,960 --> 00:04:35,000
Nevertheless, many
people stay mentally healthy.

46
00:04:35,760 --> 00:04:37,280
The question
is how?

47
00:04:40,480 --> 00:04:43,160
The largest center for resilience
research in Europe

48
00:04:43,200 --> 00:04:45,160
is in Mainz, Germany.

49
00:04:45,240 --> 00:04:49,760
Here, neuroscientist and brain
researcher Professor Raffael Kalisch

50
00:04:49,760 --> 00:04:52,680
researches the mechanisms
of mental resilience.

51
00:04:55,720 --> 00:04:57,400
What is
mental health?

52
00:04:58,160 --> 00:05:00,760
My interest in this question
comes from my very early days

53
00:05:00,760 --> 00:05:02,440
as a university
student.

54
00:05:02,880 --> 00:05:05,520
A schoolmate and close
friend of mine had a breakdown

55
00:05:05,520 --> 00:05:08,720
in the first year of his studies
while I was having a wonderful time.

56
00:05:09,320 --> 00:05:12,240
To me it was all fascinating
and great and new.

57
00:05:12,680 --> 00:05:15,320
And during that time, I
watched my friend fall apart.

58
00:05:18,240 --> 00:05:20,400
And that made
me ask myself:

59
00:05:20,400 --> 00:05:23,640
Why does it happen to some
people and not to others?

60
00:05:26,440 --> 00:05:29,360
What are the risk
factors for mental illness?

61
00:05:35,560 --> 00:05:39,400
The interesting thing is
that it’s not just really big,

62
00:05:39,400 --> 00:05:42,760
extreme life events that
can make people mentally ill.

63
00:05:44,560 --> 00:05:47,920
It’s not just a serious
accident, an act of violence,

64
00:05:47,960 --> 00:05:49,760
or the death
of a loved one,

65
00:05:50,440 --> 00:05:53,000
but also minor stresses
that can affect people

66
00:05:53,000 --> 00:05:56,840
if they occur frequently and
over an extended period of time.

67
00:06:04,400 --> 00:06:07,320
What do those with
resilience do differently?

68
00:06:07,360 --> 00:06:11,760
To find out, Kalisch is conducting
a long-term study of healthy people

69
00:06:11,800 --> 00:06:16,160
who find themselves in a particularly
difficult phase of their lives.

70
00:06:20,680 --> 00:06:23,720
We take young people who
are in this transitional phase

71
00:06:23,720 --> 00:06:26,160
from family and school to adult life,

72
00:06:26,560 --> 00:06:29,000
that are leaving a
familiar environment.

73
00:06:29,960 --> 00:06:32,720
We see that in this phase
of life, stress-related illnesses

74
00:06:32,720 --> 00:06:35,640
tend to emerge for the first
time or, if they are pre-existing,

75
00:06:35,640 --> 00:06:37,160
become more severe.

76
00:06:40,640 --> 00:06:44,440
Every three months, the 200
participants in the study answer

77
00:06:44,480 --> 00:06:47,160
a questionnaire about
their mental state.

78
00:06:50,640 --> 00:06:55,080
To what extent have you felt more
calm or tense in the past two weeks?

79
00:06:58,000 --> 00:07:02,480
Sitting on a packed train on my way
home from work was a bit stressful.

80
00:07:05,400 --> 00:07:08,280
But in the mornings, I would
occasionally just make myself

81
00:07:08,280 --> 00:07:12,960
a cup of tea and relax for ten minutes
and then I was able to settle down.

82
00:07:18,320 --> 00:07:20,280
Because we do this
every three months,

83
00:07:20,280 --> 00:07:24,240
we get a very good picture of stress
levels over a long period of time.

84
00:07:24,600 --> 00:07:27,880
So that we can see over that
time how strongly they react

85
00:07:27,880 --> 00:07:30,320
psychologically
to life’s challenges.

86
00:07:31,080 --> 00:07:34,000
Some are affected
more, others affected less.

87
00:07:36,000 --> 00:07:38,920
And in the end, this
gives us a picture of what

88
00:07:38,960 --> 00:07:42,560
mental resilience looks like
when encountering stressors.

89
00:07:46,440 --> 00:07:49,120
At regular intervals,
participants come to the institute

90
00:07:49,120 --> 00:07:50,840
for a thorough
examination.

91
00:07:53,800 --> 00:07:57,120
Kalisch and his team use MRI
machines to look for indications

92
00:07:57,120 --> 00:08:00,080
of how mental stress is
processed in the brain.

93
00:08:00,560 --> 00:08:03,480
And they examine how
stress affects the body.

94
00:08:03,840 --> 00:08:06,760
To do this, they take
blood and hair samples.

95
00:08:13,360 --> 00:08:16,840
We can measure the concentration
of the stress hormone cortisol

96
00:08:16,840 --> 00:08:18,360
from a hair sample.

97
00:08:19,880 --> 00:08:22,240
One centimeter of hair typically
corresponds

98
00:08:22,320 --> 00:08:24,080
to one month of hair growth,

99
00:08:24,520 --> 00:08:28,600
so the analysis of one centimeter,
or in this case three centimeters,

100
00:08:28,640 --> 00:08:31,880
tells us something about the
activity of the stress hormone system

101
00:08:31,880 --> 00:08:33,880
over the previous
three months.

102
00:08:36,360 --> 00:08:40,080
The results have been logged
since the study began in 2016,

103
00:08:40,160 --> 00:08:43,160
so there is now a large
database of information.

104
00:08:45,280 --> 00:08:48,280
Ultimately we want to
understand what mechanisms

105
00:08:48,360 --> 00:08:52,200
people use to stay mentally
healthy in the face of adversity.

106
00:08:56,080 --> 00:09:01,360
And once we know these mechanisms
better, to utilise or strengthen them,

107
00:09:01,360 --> 00:09:04,760
especially in people who
do not succeed in doing that.

108
00:09:09,360 --> 00:09:12,240
The study in Mainz is
due to be finished soon.

109
00:09:12,280 --> 00:09:15,880
Kalisch has already identified
certain resilience factors

110
00:09:15,920 --> 00:09:19,760
one of which is how positively
or negatively the participants

111
00:09:19,840 --> 00:09:21,640
themselves assess
their stress levels.

112
00:09:24,240 --> 00:09:26,640
There seems to be a
connection to optimism

113
00:09:26,640 --> 00:09:29,800
and the fact that people
believe they can somehow cope,

114
00:09:30,400 --> 00:09:32,920
that it will probably
work out somehow.

115
00:09:34,280 --> 00:09:36,760
That seems to be
connected to resilience,

116
00:09:36,760 --> 00:09:40,800
so someone who cultivates this
kind of positive assessment style

117
00:09:40,840 --> 00:09:44,280
or develops it over time
is less likely to be affected.

118
00:09:49,440 --> 00:09:52,120
So can we influence
how resilient we are?

119
00:09:52,160 --> 00:09:56,200
Professor and Psychiatrist Marianne
Müller is also conducting research

120
00:09:56,200 --> 00:09:58,400
at the resilience
center in Mainz,

121
00:09:58,440 --> 00:10:01,520
investigating what makes
some people particularly resilient.

122
00:10:03,600 --> 00:10:05,280
I think this
is promising

123
00:10:05,320 --> 00:10:08,360
in terms of better understanding
psychiatric illnesses.

124
00:10:08,360 --> 00:10:11,560
For many decades, we’ve
had only moderate success

125
00:10:11,560 --> 00:10:14,200
in trying to understand
how psychiatric illnesses,

126
00:10:14,200 --> 00:10:18,880
for example stress-related illnesses
such as clinical depression, develop.

127
00:10:21,920 --> 00:10:25,080
While psychiatric research focuses
primarily on the clinical picture,

128
00:10:25,720 --> 00:10:28,920
the science of resilience is more
concerned with healthy people.

129
00:10:30,480 --> 00:10:32,880
Müller is first
exploring the basics:

130
00:10:33,360 --> 00:10:35,720
What does resilient
behavior even look like?

131
00:10:38,800 --> 00:10:42,520
With her colleague Ulrich Schmitz,
she is investigating this in mice.

132
00:10:46,360 --> 00:10:49,600
Resilience can only be
measured in the context of stress.

133
00:10:50,000 --> 00:10:52,360
That’s why they
put small brown mice

134
00:10:52,400 --> 00:10:55,200
in the cage with a much larger
and stronger white mouse.

135
00:10:58,200 --> 00:11:02,440
We take male mice, which, like all
or almost all other male vertebrates,

136
00:11:02,440 --> 00:11:04,640
show territorial
behavior.

137
00:11:05,400 --> 00:11:08,920
This means that if you place a
test mouse with a larger mouse

138
00:11:08,920 --> 00:11:11,560
in its home cage, the
larger mouse won’t accept it

139
00:11:11,560 --> 00:11:14,120
and will try to scare or drive it
away.

140
00:11:14,160 --> 00:11:16,320
This leads to social stress.

141
00:11:19,120 --> 00:11:23,000
The brown mouse is removed in order
to prevent it from becoming injured.

142
00:11:23,000 --> 00:11:25,880
The experiment is
repeated for 10 days.

143
00:11:27,600 --> 00:11:30,520
They want to know:
What is the long-term effect

144
00:11:30,600 --> 00:11:33,400
of this permanent stress on the
behavior of the stressed mice?

145
00:11:34,040 --> 00:11:37,560
After a day’s break, they
undertake a second experiment:

146
00:11:38,400 --> 00:11:41,880
The brown mice are again exposed
to the white aggressor mouse.

147
00:11:41,880 --> 00:11:44,640
But this time the white
mouse is in a cage.

148
00:11:46,280 --> 00:11:48,920
The researchers now
observe the following:

149
00:11:48,960 --> 00:11:51,400
How do the previously
stressed mice behave?

150
00:11:52,000 --> 00:11:53,800
Do they stay
away fearfully?

151
00:11:53,840 --> 00:11:57,320
Are they brave enough to approach
the white aggressor in the cage?

152
00:11:58,960 --> 00:12:02,560
We assumed that the mice were
resilient if they always investigated

153
00:12:02,560 --> 00:12:07,120
the white mouse a lot, as if they
had never experienced stress.

154
00:12:10,880 --> 00:12:14,840
However, we thought this
might not be resilience at all,

155
00:12:14,840 --> 00:12:19,320
but rather the result of a less than
optimal learning process over time

156
00:12:19,960 --> 00:12:22,280
that the test mouse
has not learned,

157
00:12:22,320 --> 00:12:26,960
has not understood that this white
mouse strain is potentially dangerous.

158
00:12:27,200 --> 00:12:31,600
If that were the case, this mouse
could not be described as resilient.

159
00:12:37,720 --> 00:12:41,640
So is the intrepid mouse
perhaps just too... dumb?

160
00:12:42,320 --> 00:12:45,600
The researchers investigate
this question in a third experiment.

161
00:12:46,720 --> 00:12:49,720
Here, two large mice are
placed behind bars in the cage:

162
00:12:50,440 --> 00:12:53,120
The aggressive white
one and a brown one,

163
00:12:53,160 --> 00:12:54,680
with which the stressed little mouse

164
00:12:54,760 --> 00:12:57,320
has had no negative
experiences so far.

165
00:13:04,960 --> 00:13:08,320
Our test mouse was allowed
to freely explore the box,

166
00:13:08,360 --> 00:13:12,840
examine and visit the different social
partners and interact with them.

167
00:13:13,680 --> 00:13:16,200
And we were able to
show there are mice

168
00:13:16,280 --> 00:13:18,840
that are able to distinguish
between the white mouse,

169
00:13:18,920 --> 00:13:22,280
which comes from the aggressor
strain, and the brown mouse,

170
00:13:22,280 --> 00:13:27,200
which is neutral and with whom it
has not had any negative experiences.

171
00:13:30,040 --> 00:13:32,760
Is resilient behavior about
being able to distinguish

172
00:13:32,760 --> 00:13:34,440
between threat
and safety?

173
00:13:37,040 --> 00:13:39,920
We currently see a
resilient mouse as a mouse

174
00:13:39,960 --> 00:13:43,240
that examines the brown
mouse in a completely normal

175
00:13:43,280 --> 00:13:47,520
and unimpressed manner, but keeps
its distance from the white mouse

176
00:13:47,560 --> 00:13:51,320
because it has learned that the
white mouse is a potential threat.

177
00:13:51,320 --> 00:13:54,480
We consider this a resilient
behavioral phenotype.

178
00:13:59,480 --> 00:14:02,240
That means resilient
behavior in mice doesn’t mean

179
00:14:02,240 --> 00:14:04,840
simply bravely confronting
every impending danger

180
00:14:04,840 --> 00:14:09,320
but rather weighing up the situation
and adapting behavior accordingly.

181
00:14:17,240 --> 00:14:20,360
This is directly transferable
from mice to humans.

182
00:14:21,360 --> 00:14:24,200
There’s a lot of data showing
that people who can distinguish

183
00:14:24,200 --> 00:14:28,320
between negative stimuli and
neutral stimuli are better protected

184
00:14:28,360 --> 00:14:32,120
against stress and associated
stress-related mental illness.

185
00:14:36,640 --> 00:14:40,760
In this respect, we are also
quite confident that we can use this

186
00:14:40,800 --> 00:14:44,440
to derive further
neurobiological findings.

187
00:14:51,680 --> 00:14:52,680
Moving on.

188
00:14:53,640 --> 00:14:55,440
Finding her
way back to life

189
00:14:56,120 --> 00:15:00,800
Céline Wilke tries to do this every
day after the death of her son Freddy.

190
00:15:01,680 --> 00:15:04,400
I said goodbye for the
last time on Friday evening,

191
00:15:04,400 --> 00:15:05,680
when he told
me cheerfully.

192
00:15:05,680 --> 00:15:06,880
“Mom, I’m off.”

193
00:15:06,920 --> 00:15:08,600
I told him
to have fun.

194
00:15:08,640 --> 00:15:10,320
That was
our farewell.

195
00:15:12,880 --> 00:15:15,160
I can still see him
in that moment.

196
00:15:15,680 --> 00:15:19,600
He was in a good mood, looking forward
to the evening with his friends.

197
00:15:19,600 --> 00:15:23,160
And in retrospect, I’m
glad that the last time

198
00:15:23,160 --> 00:15:25,400
we saw each other was
really a pleasant moment

199
00:15:25,400 --> 00:15:29,200
and that we said goodbye to each
other nicely without knowing that

200
00:15:29,200 --> 00:15:31,280
we’d never see
each other again.

201
00:15:35,960 --> 00:15:39,800
On the day of the funeral, the
whole town mourned with the families.

202
00:15:42,480 --> 00:15:45,880
Luca and Freddy are buried
together in the same grave.

203
00:15:55,400 --> 00:15:58,760
And then the whole congregation
walked a good kilometer here

204
00:15:58,760 --> 00:16:01,360
from the church in
a funeral procession.

205
00:16:31,400 --> 00:16:33,320
It was like
being in a trance.

206
00:16:34,960 --> 00:16:37,840
And the burial itself,
of course you noticed

207
00:16:37,880 --> 00:16:39,960
that there were a
lot of people there,

208
00:16:39,960 --> 00:16:42,320
but it passes you
by and somehow

209
00:16:42,320 --> 00:16:44,920
you don’t really have any
clear memory of the moment.

210
00:16:52,280 --> 00:16:55,480
And the funeral, it felt
like this whole heaviness,

211
00:16:55,520 --> 00:16:57,480
this coffin with
both of them,

212
00:16:58,040 --> 00:17:02,920
I don’t know if heaviness is the right
word - this burden, which is so heavy.

213
00:17:02,960 --> 00:17:05,960
Like I already described
it, the earth opens up,

214
00:17:06,000 --> 00:17:08,560
that someone accepts
the burden for me,

215
00:17:08,600 --> 00:17:11,400
that I could now pass
it in to other hands.

216
00:17:11,440 --> 00:17:14,520
Maybe there is a bit of
religion in me after all.

217
00:17:15,400 --> 00:17:19,480
The physical part will be buried
for now, but the emotional part,

218
00:17:19,520 --> 00:17:24,280
it will take a very long time before
this wound is no longer quite so open.

219
00:17:41,320 --> 00:17:42,320
Munich.

220
00:17:43,160 --> 00:17:45,840
Here, at the Max Planck
Institute of Psychiatry,

221
00:17:45,840 --> 00:17:49,000
neuroscientist Elisabeth
Binder wants to find out:

222
00:17:49,560 --> 00:17:53,680
Why do some people remain mentally
healthy despite severe stress,

223
00:17:53,720 --> 00:17:56,640
while others become ill
under the same stress?

224
00:17:59,040 --> 00:18:02,240
One topic we researched
was genetic predisposition.

225
00:18:03,440 --> 00:18:06,240
Could it be that certain people
are genetically predisposed

226
00:18:06,240 --> 00:18:10,240
to react more or less to stress
and are therefore more or less at risk

227
00:18:10,240 --> 00:18:12,440
of negative
effects later on,

228
00:18:12,440 --> 00:18:16,080
such as depression or
post-traumatic stress disorder?

229
00:18:20,840 --> 00:18:23,240
Elisabeth Binder
wants to know:

230
00:18:23,240 --> 00:18:26,960
Could it be a gene variant that
alters our perception of stress?

231
00:18:27,680 --> 00:18:31,160
To find out, she is tracking
down the hormone cortisol,

232
00:18:31,200 --> 00:18:34,080
an important hormone for our
metabolism and immune system

233
00:18:34,080 --> 00:18:36,240
that’s also released
when we’re under stress.

234
00:18:36,920 --> 00:18:39,920
It’s regulated by something
called the HPA Axis.

235
00:18:42,840 --> 00:18:46,320
When we experience
stress, the brain is activated.

236
00:18:46,320 --> 00:18:49,080
This sets off a complex
cascade of events.

237
00:18:49,640 --> 00:18:53,320
In the brain, stress signals
are sent to the hypothalamus.

238
00:18:53,360 --> 00:18:57,360
This in turn releases hormones that
make their way to the pituitary gland.

239
00:18:59,480 --> 00:19:03,840
The pituitary gland then releases
its hormones to the adrenal cortex,

240
00:19:03,920 --> 00:19:05,320
which instructs our bodies to make
extra cortisol

241
00:19:05,360 --> 00:19:07,720
to deal with the stress.

242
00:19:10,520 --> 00:19:13,680
Cortisol allows all the
cells in our body to flood

243
00:19:13,720 --> 00:19:17,440
with additional energy to
aid the fight or flight response.

244
00:19:23,040 --> 00:19:25,440
Cortisol is our main stress hormone

245
00:19:25,520 --> 00:19:27,920
and
it travels to all organs in the body.

246
00:19:30,280 --> 00:19:34,560
It’s very important that we are
well prepared for a stressful event.

247
00:19:38,480 --> 00:19:42,000
Cortisol binds to receptors in
our cells and that’s a good thing

248
00:19:42,000 --> 00:19:44,760
Because the receptors
then report back to the brain:

249
00:19:44,760 --> 00:19:48,040
“Thank you! We have enough
cortisol bound here now.”

250
00:19:48,080 --> 00:19:50,760
The stress response is then switched
off in the brain

251
00:19:50,800 --> 00:19:52,400
and we calm down again.

252
00:19:53,120 --> 00:19:56,280
At least when everything
is functioning correctly.

253
00:20:00,480 --> 00:20:03,760
Many people are nervous
at interviews, but normally,

254
00:20:03,920 --> 00:20:05,240
when the
situation is over,

255
00:20:05,280 --> 00:20:08,520
our stress hormone levels
should downregulate again.

256
00:20:09,760 --> 00:20:13,600
People with this particular
genotype are not so good at this.

257
00:20:13,640 --> 00:20:17,160
So for them, the stress
hormone stays higher for longer.

258
00:20:20,280 --> 00:20:23,600
The question Binder
asks is: Why is that?

259
00:20:23,600 --> 00:20:26,920
Why are some people not as
good at calming down than others?

260
00:20:26,920 --> 00:20:29,640
And this is where
genetics come into play.

261
00:20:35,360 --> 00:20:39,960
One of the genes responsible for our
stress regulation is the FKBP5 gene.

262
00:20:40,960 --> 00:20:43,640
It is activated during
stress and ensures

263
00:20:43,680 --> 00:20:45,600
that an important
enzyme is released.

264
00:20:45,600 --> 00:20:49,840
It has the same name as its
corresponding gene - fkbp5

265
00:20:53,560 --> 00:20:56,680
The problems start when
too much of it is released

266
00:21:00,040 --> 00:21:04,560
Then the enzyme wedges its way between
the stress receptor and the cortisol,

267
00:21:04,560 --> 00:21:09,080
and thus blocks the stress
receptors’ message to the brain

268
00:21:13,840 --> 00:21:15,800
that there is
enough cortisol.

269
00:21:15,840 --> 00:21:19,240
The brain is misinformed - it keeps
firing and we can no longer calm down.

270
00:21:23,240 --> 00:21:27,120
We think that stress
causes too much of this fkbp5

271
00:21:27,120 --> 00:21:29,440
to be released and that
people simply release

272
00:21:29,440 --> 00:21:33,080
too much of the stress hormone
after even the slightest stress.

273
00:21:33,080 --> 00:21:35,680
And we know that too
much of this stress hormone

274
00:21:35,680 --> 00:21:39,360
in the long term is bad for many
processes including in the brain,

275
00:21:39,360 --> 00:21:42,800
which also increases the
risk of psychiatric illnesses.

276
00:21:45,520 --> 00:21:48,960
The researchers have
identified the FKBP5 gene

277
00:21:48,960 --> 00:21:52,840
as one of several important causes
of our hormonal stress regulation.

278
00:21:55,800 --> 00:21:58,880
Variants in this gene
could be partly responsible

279
00:21:58,920 --> 00:22:01,640
for why we react with
more or less stress.

280
00:22:02,560 --> 00:22:04,560
The researchers are
now looking for a way

281
00:22:04,560 --> 00:22:07,880
to block activity directly
at the FKBP5 gene.

282
00:22:12,200 --> 00:22:15,680
And here at the institute, this
has been investigated in mice

283
00:22:15,680 --> 00:22:18,920
that have been given
this FKBP5 blocker

284
00:22:19,200 --> 00:22:21,480
whether they are
more resilient to stress

285
00:22:21,480 --> 00:22:23,240
and are better
able to cope with it

286
00:22:23,240 --> 00:22:26,440
to the extent that this can
be measured in a mouse.

287
00:22:30,680 --> 00:22:34,320
The next step is to develop
a drug specifically for people

288
00:22:34,360 --> 00:22:37,680
in whom this stress gene variant
can be detected in the blood,

289
00:22:38,920 --> 00:22:43,080
and who therefore presumably
have a higher risk of mental illness.

290
00:22:57,760 --> 00:22:59,280
The South
of France.

291
00:22:59,560 --> 00:23:04,560
Psychiatrist and neurologist Boris
Cyrulnik lives and works in Toulon.

292
00:23:05,200 --> 00:23:08,280
He’s considered a pioneer
of resilience research.

293
00:23:09,960 --> 00:23:12,440
The traumatic experiences
of his own childhood

294
00:23:12,520 --> 00:23:14,560
give him a personal
perspective.

295
00:23:16,640 --> 00:23:18,680
I had a somewhat
difficult childhood,

296
00:23:18,680 --> 00:23:23,040
which motivated me to go into research
because I wanted to understand

297
00:23:23,080 --> 00:23:25,960
how we can find our way
back into life after war,

298
00:23:25,960 --> 00:23:29,160
as I had lost almost my
entire family in Auschwitz.

299
00:23:32,480 --> 00:23:37,160
As a four-year-old, Boris was left on
his own, without a mother or father.

300
00:23:37,880 --> 00:23:39,560
Hidden by
foster families,

301
00:23:39,600 --> 00:23:43,000
he was eventually arrested
and narrowly escaped death.

302
00:23:44,520 --> 00:23:47,440
The officers said:
he should be killed.

303
00:23:48,000 --> 00:23:51,320
So I knew they wanted to
kill me and I was all alone.

304
00:23:51,360 --> 00:23:56,040
And I heard adults say: He
has no family, he is lost to life.

305
00:23:56,640 --> 00:23:59,520
When I was a child, I
thought: This can’t be true.

306
00:23:59,520 --> 00:24:01,040
I reject
this curse!

307
00:24:05,480 --> 00:24:06,640
At the age of 11,

308
00:24:06,680 --> 00:24:10,520
Cyrulnik already knew that he
wanted to become a psychiatrist.

309
00:24:11,560 --> 00:24:14,400
After the war, he
studied medicine in Paris.

310
00:24:15,000 --> 00:24:18,960
The memory of his loving parents
also helped him to forge his own path.

311
00:24:22,160 --> 00:24:25,400
I fought to study, even
though everyone told me:

312
00:24:25,400 --> 00:24:28,200
Don’t bother studying, you
won’t make it.

313
00:24:28,480 --> 00:24:29,920
Well, I made it.

314
00:24:32,720 --> 00:24:35,840
When the trained psychiatrist
came across the term resilience

315
00:24:35,840 --> 00:24:38,720
for the first time, he
found his life purpose.

316
00:24:42,560 --> 00:24:46,160
When I came across the
word resilience, I said to myself,

317
00:24:46,200 --> 00:24:49,240
this is a word that needs to
be developed scientifically,

318
00:24:49,240 --> 00:24:53,400
and we need to bring it into our
culture to make people understand

319
00:24:53,600 --> 00:24:57,680
that if we abandon injured
people, there will be no resilience.

320
00:25:01,440 --> 00:25:05,680
Due to his own painful experience,
one focus of Cyrulnik’s research

321
00:25:05,720 --> 00:25:07,240
is child
protection.

322
00:25:08,280 --> 00:25:12,880
He’s particularly interested in the
interaction between mother and child.

323
00:25:16,000 --> 00:25:20,800
We started our research into
early childhood in 1981 on the island

324
00:25:20,800 --> 00:25:25,520
Embiez, near Toulon, where we gathered
a team of international researchers.

325
00:25:27,560 --> 00:25:32,160
The question was: What makes
one child more resistant to crisis,

326
00:25:32,160 --> 00:25:34,680
while another is
very vulnerable to it?

327
00:25:39,000 --> 00:25:42,640
He sees one of the reasons for
this in the mothers of these children.

328
00:25:42,960 --> 00:25:46,800
If the mother is not doing well, her
relationship with her child suffers

329
00:25:47,440 --> 00:25:50,680
This is a vicious circle that
can begin during pregnancy.

330
00:25:53,080 --> 00:25:55,760
We have been able to
scientifically demonstrate

331
00:25:55,760 --> 00:26:00,360
that when a pregnant woman is stressed
the stress hormones enter the uterus

332
00:26:00,360 --> 00:26:03,800
and the baby swallows stress
hormones such as cortisol,

333
00:26:04,200 --> 00:26:06,600
which are harmful
to the baby’s brain

334
00:26:12,480 --> 00:26:15,920
This means that the baby is
born with cognitive changes

335
00:26:15,960 --> 00:26:20,200
that are not caused by the mother,
but by the mother’s unhappiness.

336
00:26:26,840 --> 00:26:30,200
The researchers identified
a particularly sensitive phase

337
00:26:30,200 --> 00:26:33,720
in the final weeks of pregnancy and
the first two years of a child’s life.

338
00:26:36,400 --> 00:26:40,200
New networks are constantly being
formed in the brain during this time.

339
00:26:52,760 --> 00:26:56,360
If nobody does anything,
it’s a neurological catastrophe.

340
00:26:56,880 --> 00:26:58,400
The brain
atrophies.

341
00:26:58,440 --> 00:27:03,480
But we can intervene, we can intervene
gently very early and easily.

342
00:27:03,480 --> 00:27:07,040
And the earlier we intervene,
the easier it is to trigger

343
00:27:07,080 --> 00:27:08,800
the resilience
process.

344
00:27:09,760 --> 00:27:13,800
But if the child is left very
isolated for a long time,

345
00:27:13,800 --> 00:27:16,640
the resilience process
will be difficult to initiate.

346
00:27:31,240 --> 00:27:35,600
Elisabeth Binder is also studying this
particularly vulnerable phase of life

347
00:27:35,600 --> 00:27:37,600
at the Max Planck
Institute in Munich.

348
00:27:41,320 --> 00:27:44,200
The biggest correlation
is an almost 40-fold

349
00:27:44,240 --> 00:27:47,800
risk of having attempted suicide
when someone experiences

350
00:27:47,880 --> 00:27:50,480
severe traumatic
experiences as a child.

351
00:27:52,920 --> 00:27:55,920
Even during pregnancy,
too much stress or trauma

352
00:27:55,960 --> 00:27:58,160
can lead the embryo’s
natural barrier against

353
00:27:58,160 --> 00:28:00,400
the mother’s stress
hormones to break.

354
00:28:02,920 --> 00:28:05,720
Why is chronic stress
harmful to the brain?

355
00:28:05,800 --> 00:28:10,080
Because chronic stress leads to
our neural circuits becoming weaker.

356
00:28:13,520 --> 00:28:17,040
Binder suspects the stress
hormone cortisol causes this.

357
00:28:17,400 --> 00:28:19,520
But to prove it, she
would have to be able

358
00:28:19,520 --> 00:28:21,920
to examine the brain of
an embryo under stress.

359
00:28:25,320 --> 00:28:28,760
It’s difficult of course to
get to the developing brain

360
00:28:28,840 --> 00:28:33,000
and to expose it to certain factors
and investigate how it reacts.

361
00:28:36,360 --> 00:28:38,960
Because this obviously
is not an option,

362
00:28:39,040 --> 00:28:41,440
the researchers are using
a novel method to recreate

363
00:28:41,440 --> 00:28:43,800
a developing brain
outside the womb.

364
00:28:45,040 --> 00:28:47,840
A brain-like cell structure
is grown from stem cells.

365
00:28:50,440 --> 00:28:53,840
The researchers call this
simulation a brain organoid.

366
00:29:00,640 --> 00:29:04,160
We can only properly model
very early brain development.

367
00:29:04,200 --> 00:29:06,920
We can’t model all
cell types in the brain

368
00:29:07,000 --> 00:29:10,200
and we can’t model how different
brain areas talk to each other.

369
00:29:10,200 --> 00:29:12,600
So it’s a very simple
and limited model,

370
00:29:12,600 --> 00:29:15,400
but it’s the first time
we’ve been able to do this.

371
00:29:17,600 --> 00:29:20,200
How old are these
organoids now?

372
00:29:20,200 --> 00:29:22,320
These ones are
about 40 days old.

373
00:29:23,240 --> 00:29:26,000
40 days old. So that
means we could begin.

374
00:29:26,040 --> 00:29:28,640
Yes, this would be
a good time to start.

375
00:29:29,600 --> 00:29:33,080
Once the simulated embryonic
brain - the brain organoid

376
00:29:33,120 --> 00:29:35,840
has reached a certain
stage of maturation,

377
00:29:35,920 --> 00:29:39,440
Binder and her team add
synthetically produced cortisol.

378
00:29:41,920 --> 00:29:43,880
The effects of the hormone
on the development

379
00:29:43,880 --> 00:29:46,080
of the brain cells
are then investigated.

380
00:29:54,960 --> 00:29:57,800
And then we see that this
stress hormone actually alters

381
00:29:57,800 --> 00:29:59,560
the development
and, in particular,

382
00:29:59,560 --> 00:30:02,120
the gene expression of
genes that have also been

383
00:30:02,120 --> 00:30:05,240
associated with the risk
of psychiatric illnesses.

384
00:30:08,560 --> 00:30:11,400
There’s no doubt about
it: Even in the womb,

385
00:30:11,480 --> 00:30:14,960
the mother’s stress influences how
strongly genes manifest themselves

386
00:30:14,960 --> 00:30:19,280
in the child and can therefore have a
lasting effect on their mental health.

387
00:30:20,040 --> 00:30:23,120
A finding that could
have real life implications.

388
00:30:23,640 --> 00:30:29,120
It’s also important to screen for
psychiatric symptoms during pregnancy.

389
00:30:29,120 --> 00:30:31,840
Some clinics already
screen for depression

390
00:30:31,880 --> 00:30:33,640
and treat the
mother in good time.

391
00:30:35,800 --> 00:30:38,400
There are now many
studies experimenting

392
00:30:38,480 --> 00:30:42,360
with using therapy during
pregnancy to possibly mitigate risk.

393
00:30:50,720 --> 00:30:54,680
Environmental influences therefore
have a direct impact on our genes.

394
00:30:55,680 --> 00:30:59,240
Scientist and psychiatrist
Katharina Domschke in Freiburg

395
00:30:59,320 --> 00:31:02,200
is investigating exactly
what this looks like.

396
00:31:07,720 --> 00:31:11,320
We think that environmental
influences can trigger illnesses.

397
00:31:11,320 --> 00:31:15,000
So now the question is, how does
the environment affect our genes?

398
00:31:16,760 --> 00:31:21,000
Domschke is Head of Psychiatry at the
University of Freiburg Medical Center.

399
00:31:21,480 --> 00:31:25,000
In her laboratory, she
investigates epigenetic processes

400
00:31:25,440 --> 00:31:28,000
how and why changes
occur in our genes.

401
00:31:32,600 --> 00:31:36,000
Let’s imagine we have a
certain genetic predisposition

402
00:31:36,040 --> 00:31:39,440
and there is a particular
adverse environmental event.

403
00:31:40,120 --> 00:31:43,560
But how does this environmental
event cause our genes to express,

404
00:31:43,560 --> 00:31:47,600
or  trigger, so to speak,
and ultimately lead to illness.

405
00:31:48,200 --> 00:31:50,680
This is where
epigenetics play a role.

406
00:31:53,120 --> 00:31:56,440
In order to understand this
communication between environment

407
00:31:56,440 --> 00:31:59,360
and genes in more detail,
Domschke and her team

408
00:31:59,440 --> 00:32:01,840
are looking at another stress gene

409
00:32:01,920 --> 00:32:04,040
the MAOA gene.

410
00:32:09,120 --> 00:32:11,080
It provides
instructions to make an enzyme

411
00:32:11,160 --> 00:32:13,000
also called maoa.

412
00:32:16,440 --> 00:32:18,720
This enzyme migrates
into our nerve cells

413
00:32:18,720 --> 00:32:21,280
and attaches itself
to the synaptic clefts,

414
00:32:21,320 --> 00:32:24,080
the area between
the nerve cells.

415
00:32:24,800 --> 00:32:29,040
This is where our happiness hormones
- serotonin and norepinephrine

416
00:32:29,080 --> 00:32:32,080
are normally transported from
cell membrane to cell membrane.

417
00:32:33,400 --> 00:32:36,840
However, if too much of the
maoa enzyme attaches itself

418
00:32:36,880 --> 00:32:38,320
to the synaptic cleft,

419
00:32:38,400 --> 00:32:41,120
it turns out to be a
real happiness eater:

420
00:32:41,160 --> 00:32:44,760
it simply degrades away the
serotonin and norepinephrine.

421
00:32:46,080 --> 00:32:49,920
one of the main suspects in
the development of mental illness

422
00:32:49,960 --> 00:32:54,400
is monoamine oxidase
A, or MAO-A for short.

423
00:32:54,400 --> 00:32:58,600
This is because MAO-A breaks
down norepinephrine and serotonin.

424
00:33:00,040 --> 00:33:03,640
But in some of us, this
happiness-eating Maoa enzyme

425
00:33:03,640 --> 00:33:05,560
is more active
than in others.

426
00:33:05,600 --> 00:33:06,600
Why is that

427
00:33:07,880 --> 00:33:11,240
Domschke takes blood samples
to monitor the MAOA gene

428
00:33:11,280 --> 00:33:13,960
and see how much
methylation has occured.

429
00:33:15,760 --> 00:33:18,400
Methylation is a chemical process

430
00:33:18,440 --> 00:33:19,920
where a methane
derivative binds itself

431
00:33:19,920 --> 00:33:22,920
to specific sites on our
DNA, deactivating them.

432
00:33:23,880 --> 00:33:27,080
This can be imagined as a
kind of cap that sits on our genes,

433
00:33:27,080 --> 00:33:28,320
putting them
to sleep.

434
00:33:29,280 --> 00:33:32,360
If the cap sits on the gene,
it is considered methylated,

435
00:33:32,360 --> 00:33:35,360
and the gene is dormant,
silencing its activity.

436
00:33:38,760 --> 00:33:42,280
If the cap is removed,
however, it becomes active again.

437
00:33:43,200 --> 00:33:47,280
In the case of the MAOA gene,
more happiness eaters are produced.

438
00:33:50,240 --> 00:33:54,160
The MAO-A enzyme is more active
and breaks down more serotonin

439
00:33:54,200 --> 00:33:55,720
and norepinephrine.

440
00:33:56,240 --> 00:33:59,840
There’s less of those hormones
available in the synaptic cleft

441
00:33:59,840 --> 00:34:03,200
between the nerve cells and
it may be easier for depression

442
00:34:03,200 --> 00:34:04,880
and anxiety
to develop.

443
00:34:04,960 --> 00:34:07,680
This means that we
are in a state of risk.

444
00:34:10,000 --> 00:34:12,480
If these caps are
on our stress genes,

445
00:34:12,520 --> 00:34:15,400
they protect us from producing
too many happiness eaters.

446
00:34:16,360 --> 00:34:18,480
This can make us
happier and more resilient.

447
00:34:19,560 --> 00:34:22,040
But that doesn’t always
happen for everyone.

448
00:34:26,400 --> 00:34:29,640
What we saw was
that negative life events

449
00:34:29,640 --> 00:34:33,440
were more likely to lead to the
MAO-A being less methylated,

450
00:34:33,880 --> 00:34:37,840
i.e. having fewer caps on the
gene, and possibly being at risk,

451
00:34:38,400 --> 00:34:41,200
while positive life
events were more likely

452
00:34:41,240 --> 00:34:44,120
to be associated with
increased methylation.

453
00:34:45,040 --> 00:34:48,800
So that means the caps were
more likely to be on the MAO-A gene,

454
00:34:48,800 --> 00:34:50,800
possibly indicating
resilience.

455
00:34:53,720 --> 00:34:55,400
Positive environmental
influences

456
00:34:55,400 --> 00:34:58,560
and experiences can affect
how our genes are expressed.

457
00:34:59,720 --> 00:35:01,440
Domschke’s
next question is

458
00:35:01,480 --> 00:35:05,200
can this also be demonstrated
in successful use of therapy?

459
00:35:08,360 --> 00:35:12,880
We’ve known for a long time
that psychotherapy works.

460
00:35:12,880 --> 00:35:15,480
Psychotherapy is one of
the most effective treatments

461
00:35:15,480 --> 00:35:16,920
anxiety disorders.

462
00:35:17,000 --> 00:35:20,000
What we don’t yet know
is how it works in detail

463
00:35:20,040 --> 00:35:22,080
And based on our
research findings,

464
00:35:22,080 --> 00:35:26,200
one possible mechanism could
be working at the cell nucleus level

465
00:35:30,880 --> 00:35:33,800
Can we put these caps
back on the stress genes

466
00:35:33,840 --> 00:35:35,320
with the help of
psychotherapy?

467
00:35:36,360 --> 00:35:39,440
Domschke examines the blood of
patients who are afraid of heights

468
00:35:40,040 --> 00:35:42,440
she climbs the tower
of the Freiburg Cathedral

469
00:35:42,440 --> 00:35:43,880
with them every
day for two weeks.

470
00:35:49,920 --> 00:35:54,200
The patients did what we
call exposure exercises,

471
00:35:54,200 --> 00:35:57,360
where they exposed
themselves to their fear of heights

472
00:35:57,360 --> 00:36:01,960
So they went up the tower, had
to look down and after the therapy

473
00:36:02,040 --> 00:36:03,840
we took blood
samples again.

474
00:36:04,440 --> 00:36:07,600
And what we saw was that in
patients with a successful response

475
00:36:07,600 --> 00:36:11,160
to psychotherapy, MAOA
methylation had returned

476
00:36:11,160 --> 00:36:14,040
to the level of the
healthy control subjects.

477
00:36:16,960 --> 00:36:20,560
Domschke also obtained the same
result in a study on psychotherapy.

478
00:36:21,640 --> 00:36:24,320
The number of test
subjects is still too small

479
00:36:24,320 --> 00:36:27,960
to make a definitive statement,
but the initial results are promising.

480
00:36:30,520 --> 00:36:34,560
So there are stress gene variants
that we bring into the world with us,

481
00:36:34,560 --> 00:36:38,000
and yet we can have a major
influence on our resilience

482
00:36:38,080 --> 00:36:41,440
if we manage to shape our
environment consciously and well.

483
00:37:02,840 --> 00:37:05,960
At first, I was paralyzed,
I couldn’t do anything.

484
00:37:07,280 --> 00:37:11,680
The will to keep going was gone,
you’re no longer capable of anything.

485
00:37:12,640 --> 00:37:14,160
That was at
the beginning.

486
00:37:14,160 --> 00:37:15,920
And then
things got better.

487
00:37:15,960 --> 00:37:18,400
Music was like a
prayer, a meditation,

488
00:37:18,480 --> 00:37:21,120
a connection that
helped me tremendously.

489
00:37:29,280 --> 00:37:33,720
A lot of people might ask, where is
God, but that didn’t happen to me.

490
00:37:33,760 --> 00:37:38,400
For me, music was my religion,
and my family and my friends.

491
00:37:38,400 --> 00:37:41,000
That was what saved
me during that time.

492
00:38:10,400 --> 00:38:14,440
Luca’s father, Georg Ballmann,
often considers going to therapy.

493
00:38:15,600 --> 00:38:19,240
The fact that his son had to
die, the senselessness of it,

494
00:38:19,280 --> 00:38:21,160
almost brings
him to despair.

495
00:38:24,280 --> 00:38:28,080
When you think about the trivial
reason that led to this terrible end,

496
00:38:28,080 --> 00:38:29,760
you just don’t
understand

497
00:38:29,800 --> 00:38:31,320
It’s so
meaningless.

498
00:38:36,600 --> 00:38:40,280
Georg Ballmann wants to do
something about that meaninglessness.

499
00:38:43,440 --> 00:38:47,240
And that’s why we very quickly had
the idea of setting up a foundation

500
00:38:47,240 --> 00:38:50,280
to turn this  meaninglessness
into something meaningful.

501
00:38:55,560 --> 00:38:58,840
Together with Freddy’s
parents, Ballmann established

502
00:38:58,880 --> 00:39:02,920
the “faustlos” foundation, a
program that starts in kindergarten

503
00:39:02,960 --> 00:39:04,920
to prevent violence
as early as possible.

504
00:39:05,920 --> 00:39:10,160
It allows him to stay active, and
keep the memory of his son alive.

505
00:39:17,000 --> 00:39:21,200
At the Mainz resilience center,
psychologist Michèle Wessa focuses

506
00:39:21,200 --> 00:39:25,040
her research on very practical
help for people in crisis situations.

507
00:39:26,000 --> 00:39:30,120
She says that resilient behavior
can only develop very gradually.

508
00:39:31,440 --> 00:39:35,720
It doesn’t work to develop training
courses that somehow make people

509
00:39:35,760 --> 00:39:38,040
more resilient and more efficient

510
00:39:38,080 --> 00:39:40,440
within an hour and
a half or even a day,

511
00:39:40,480 --> 00:39:43,760
which is perhaps what
some people would like to see.

512
00:39:43,800 --> 00:39:46,880
That won’t work and
that’s not what we aim to do.

513
00:39:47,920 --> 00:39:50,840
For Wessa, resilience
is regulated by the self.

514
00:39:52,760 --> 00:39:56,080
She tells us her favorite story
to help explain what she means.

515
00:40:02,800 --> 00:40:06,440
The story of the elephant
in chains by Jorge Bucay

516
00:40:06,440 --> 00:40:09,160
is about a very small
elephant, a newborn,

517
00:40:09,200 --> 00:40:12,200
being chained to a small
wooden peg in a circus.

518
00:40:15,600 --> 00:40:18,840
The baby elephant keeps
trying to break free from the chain,

519
00:40:18,880 --> 00:40:20,440
but to no avail.

520
00:40:21,880 --> 00:40:25,800
But it eventually grew
bigger and stronger, in fact,

521
00:40:25,800 --> 00:40:28,080
it could have
broken free long ago.

522
00:40:29,240 --> 00:40:31,720
It lacks the belief
that it can do it,

523
00:40:31,760 --> 00:40:35,960
and that alone causes it to stand
still and not try to free itself.

524
00:40:37,720 --> 00:40:40,240
When we have the feeling
that we can do nothing,

525
00:40:40,480 --> 00:40:43,560
that we are powerless
and at the mercy of others,

526
00:40:43,600 --> 00:40:47,000
this is known in psychology
as “learned helplessness”.

527
00:40:49,240 --> 00:40:52,960
Wessa examines this state in
detail in various experiments.

528
00:40:57,160 --> 00:41:02,120
In the experiment, the test subjects
are first given an unpleasant noise

529
00:41:02,120 --> 00:41:06,280
and at the same time a somewhat
unpleasant stimulus on their skin.

530
00:41:06,320 --> 00:41:09,200
They are shown circles,
triangles and squares,

531
00:41:09,280 --> 00:41:11,320
on which they have to
press a different button.

532
00:41:16,160 --> 00:41:18,720
If the participants
press the right button,

533
00:41:18,720 --> 00:41:20,840
they can stop the
unpleasant noises.

534
00:41:21,360 --> 00:41:23,040
This applies
to one group.

535
00:41:26,080 --> 00:41:27,800
In a second
group, however,

536
00:41:27,840 --> 00:41:31,240
pressing the right button
sometimes causes the noises to stop,

537
00:41:31,240 --> 00:41:32,920
but sometimes
it doesn’t.

538
00:41:38,400 --> 00:41:41,400
What one group learns
is, I press a correct button,

539
00:41:41,400 --> 00:41:44,960
so I have the situation under
control, the stimulus is over.

540
00:41:44,960 --> 00:41:47,960
The other group learns
that no matter what I do,

541
00:41:48,040 --> 00:41:50,040
chance determines
what happens.

542
00:41:52,120 --> 00:41:54,480
If this happens
several times in a row,

543
00:41:54,480 --> 00:41:56,920
it leads to an experience
of helplessness

544
00:41:57,480 --> 00:41:59,200
there is nothing
we can do.

545
00:42:02,000 --> 00:42:04,600
We might recognize
this from our working life:

546
00:42:04,600 --> 00:42:07,720
I do a certain task and I
always do it the same way.

547
00:42:07,720 --> 00:42:09,360
One day the boss
is very happy,

548
00:42:09,360 --> 00:42:12,880
the next day he throws it
back at me and says it’s terrible.

549
00:42:12,880 --> 00:42:16,840
In other words, I have the feeling
that I have no control over it.

550
00:42:16,840 --> 00:42:21,040
And that’s a very important aspect
of how stressed I actually feel.

551
00:42:22,320 --> 00:42:26,400
How does the experience of
losing control affect future behavior?

552
00:42:27,720 --> 00:42:31,160
In a follow-up experiment,
the two groups can only stop

553
00:42:31,200 --> 00:42:34,600
the unpleasant noise by
finding the safe green squares

554
00:42:34,640 --> 00:42:36,360
on a field as
quickly as possible.

555
00:42:37,880 --> 00:42:41,160
Do the two groups behave differently
when searching for these squares?

556
00:42:42,680 --> 00:42:45,880
The result is that people who
have already felt they were in control

557
00:42:45,880 --> 00:42:49,480
in the previous experiment find
these safe green places much faster

558
00:42:49,480 --> 00:42:52,120
than the group that
experienced a loss of control.

559
00:43:01,280 --> 00:43:05,760
This experience that I have never
been able to do this before also leads

560
00:43:05,760 --> 00:43:09,240
to passivity and something
like the story with the elephant:

561
00:43:10,120 --> 00:43:12,760
That I just give up,
I submit to my fate.

562
00:43:13,240 --> 00:43:15,880
And so I move further
and further into this cycle

563
00:43:15,880 --> 00:43:17,800
of helplessness
and passivity.

564
00:43:22,120 --> 00:43:26,480
According to Wessa, when we manage
to free ourselves from this cycle,

565
00:43:26,520 --> 00:43:28,720
we can effect
change in our lives.

566
00:43:29,960 --> 00:43:33,160
But what about the situations
in life that we cannot change?

567
00:43:34,040 --> 00:43:38,480
I may not be able to change the actual
situation that triggered the stress,

568
00:43:38,480 --> 00:43:42,160
but I can always change something
about how I react to the stress.

569
00:43:42,160 --> 00:43:45,280
And I think it’s really
important that I learn for myself

570
00:43:45,280 --> 00:43:48,360
that although I don’t always
have control over the stressor,

571
00:43:48,360 --> 00:43:51,880
I do have some control
over my reaction to it.

572
00:43:55,080 --> 00:43:57,680
Wessa puts her research
findings into practice

573
00:43:57,680 --> 00:44:00,080
at a school in Bad
Dürckheim, Germany

574
00:44:00,840 --> 00:44:04,320
She is conducting a WHO-sponsored
resilience training program

575
00:44:04,320 --> 00:44:05,920
in a 7th
grade class.

576
00:44:07,320 --> 00:44:10,560
Mental stress has increased
significantly in recent years,

577
00:44:10,560 --> 00:44:13,440
particularly among
children and young adults,

578
00:44:13,520 --> 00:44:15,400
largely due to the
Covid pandemic.

579
00:44:20,000 --> 00:44:24,280
For me it’s important that we give
them strategies at an early stage

580
00:44:24,360 --> 00:44:27,800
that they can use themselves
to protect their mental health.

581
00:44:31,880 --> 00:44:34,640
She works with the students
on how they can free themselves

582
00:44:34,640 --> 00:44:38,040
from a stressful spiral of
negative thoughts and feelings.

583
00:44:38,040 --> 00:44:41,800
First, she asks the
question: What happens

584
00:44:41,800 --> 00:44:44,640
when we are convinced
that we will fail at something?

585
00:44:46,160 --> 00:44:48,560
Your thoughts
influence your actions.

586
00:44:48,600 --> 00:44:50,280
If you think you can’t do it,

587
00:44:50,360 --> 00:44:53,120
then it may well be
that you really can’t do it.

588
00:44:55,000 --> 00:44:59,160
So you would then say yes, I can’t
do it anyway, so I won’t even try.

589
00:45:01,600 --> 00:45:03,880
If negative thoughts
lead to bad results,

590
00:45:03,880 --> 00:45:06,440
shouldn’t the reverse
also be possible?

591
00:45:12,440 --> 00:45:14,680
That’s precisely where we can do
something

592
00:45:14,760 --> 00:45:16,800
for our health and resilience,

593
00:45:16,800 --> 00:45:20,880
namely by looking more closely at
things that we have already achieved.

594
00:45:20,880 --> 00:45:25,120
We often pay much more attention
to the things we haven’t achieved.

595
00:45:25,160 --> 00:45:27,480
And we remember
things that went wrong.

596
00:45:27,480 --> 00:45:31,240
And we somehow forget even
the little things that have gone well.

597
00:45:35,560 --> 00:45:39,480
She does a simple exercise for this
by asking the students to remember

598
00:45:39,480 --> 00:45:41,720
what they did particularly
well over the weekend

599
00:45:42,080 --> 00:45:43,960
even if it’s
something small.

600
00:45:47,000 --> 00:45:50,840
It was my grandma’s birthday last
weekend and I baked her a cake.

601
00:45:50,840 --> 00:45:54,000
I’m not actually that good at
baking, but I managed to do it.

602
00:45:54,000 --> 00:45:56,040
She really liked it and
that made me happy.

603
00:45:56,760 --> 00:45:59,520
Great, and it made
your grandma happy too.

604
00:46:01,240 --> 00:46:04,200
I helped my dad tidy up
my room on the weekend.

605
00:46:04,560 --> 00:46:05,840
I never do that!

606
00:46:06,400 --> 00:46:09,080
I always throw everything
in drawers and that’s it.

607
00:46:09,560 --> 00:46:12,160
But this time I really
did it with some structure

608
00:46:12,160 --> 00:46:15,600
and it was much, much better than
just throwing everything in somewhere.

609
00:46:18,640 --> 00:46:23,280
Remembering the successes, trying not
to judge experiences too negatively,

610
00:46:24,440 --> 00:46:28,960
staying active despite adversity -
these are key factors for resilience.

611
00:46:31,120 --> 00:46:32,640
We’ve
learned a lot.

612
00:46:33,440 --> 00:46:37,920
I understand the term now, and know
more about the topic of resilience.

613
00:46:43,360 --> 00:46:47,600
We also learned how we
feel and that we can influence

614
00:46:47,600 --> 00:46:49,560
what is happening inside of us:

615
00:46:49,640 --> 00:46:52,400
our fears and how we
can fight against them.

616
00:46:52,440 --> 00:46:54,720
And yes, it was a
great experience.

617
00:46:57,160 --> 00:47:00,480
We can equip ourselves mentally
at an early age to cope better

618
00:47:00,480 --> 00:47:05,120
with crises later on, which we will
all experience in one form or another.

619
00:47:07,520 --> 00:47:10,880
But to what extent does being
resilient also mean social pressure

620
00:47:10,880 --> 00:47:12,160
to self-optimize

621
00:47:12,200 --> 00:47:14,520
to be ready to
perform at all times?

622
00:47:16,760 --> 00:47:20,960
Resilience simply means
finding ways to deal with stress.

623
00:47:23,040 --> 00:47:26,560
And that doesn’t mean that you
should try to cope with as much stress

624
00:47:26,560 --> 00:47:30,440
as possible, but that you should
perhaps recognize for yourself:

625
00:47:30,440 --> 00:47:33,960
Well, if I have ten appointments
a day, that’s too much.

626
00:47:34,000 --> 00:47:36,320
I’d rather try to have
fewer appointments,

627
00:47:36,320 --> 00:47:39,120
or I will plan time to
relax to compensate.

628
00:47:39,160 --> 00:47:41,560
And that increases
overall resilience

629
00:47:41,600 --> 00:47:44,920
to be able to cope better
with the various factors in life.

630
00:47:51,760 --> 00:47:56,360
In Toulon, resilience researcher
Boris Cyrulnik also sees government

631
00:47:56,360 --> 00:47:59,600
and society as having a
duty to create an environment

632
00:47:59,600 --> 00:48:01,840
that enables us to
remain mentally healthy.

633
00:48:06,400 --> 00:48:11,040
Resilience is genetic,
biological, emotional and political.

634
00:48:11,040 --> 00:48:14,080
Because it’s politicians who
are going to make the decisions

635
00:48:14,080 --> 00:48:18,160
to keep pregnant women safe, to
create jobs in early childhood care.

636
00:48:18,440 --> 00:48:20,640
These are all
political decisions.

637
00:48:25,080 --> 00:48:28,880
In 2019, the French government
launched a nationwide program

638
00:48:28,920 --> 00:48:30,600
based on
Cyrulnik’s work.

639
00:48:34,280 --> 00:48:37,400
I’m very happy to welcome you
here and to launch this mission

640
00:48:37,400 --> 00:48:39,640
for children’s first
thousand days.

641
00:48:44,440 --> 00:48:46,840
And I’d like to
thank Boris Cyrulnik,

642
00:48:46,880 --> 00:48:49,760
since this work is
based on his reflections.

643
00:48:54,440 --> 00:48:56,320
The first 1000 days

644
00:48:56,320 --> 00:48:58,360
is the name of the
government program.

645
00:48:58,360 --> 00:49:00,520
Under the
leadership of Cyrulnik,

646
00:49:00,560 --> 00:49:03,760
projects are financed
nationwide in maternity wards

647
00:49:03,800 --> 00:49:07,560
and kindergartens to better protect
children and soon-to-be parents.

648
00:49:15,640 --> 00:49:19,440
At the Nymphenburg Palace in Munich,
the Ballmann and Wilke families

649
00:49:19,440 --> 00:49:22,400
are awarded the Bavarian
Innovation Prize for Volunteering

650
00:49:22,400 --> 00:49:24,320
their “faustlos” foundation

651
00:49:24,360 --> 00:49:25,480
it’s a great honor.

652
00:49:28,760 --> 00:49:32,160
And they can fund new projects
with the 10,000 euros prize money.

653
00:49:33,040 --> 00:49:36,000
Working for the foundation
means a lot to Céline Wilke.

654
00:49:37,160 --> 00:49:40,400
It’s my way of dealing with the
days when I’m not feeling great,

655
00:49:40,600 --> 00:49:42,560
when my husband
isn’t feeling great.

656
00:49:42,560 --> 00:49:44,760
That we have
something to work on.

657
00:49:46,760 --> 00:49:49,280
Their lives can never
go back to how they were.

658
00:49:49,800 --> 00:49:51,720
The scars will
remain forever.

659
00:49:52,720 --> 00:49:55,520
But the families carry
on as best they can.

660
00:50:00,760 --> 00:50:03,680
A loss like that puts
a lot into perspective.

661
00:50:04,680 --> 00:50:08,320
I don’t think you get upset about
the small things so quickly anymore.

662
00:50:08,320 --> 00:50:12,280
You enjoy the moment more because you
know how quickly moments can change,

663
00:50:12,280 --> 00:50:13,960
how situations
can change.

664
00:50:14,520 --> 00:50:17,440
You move through life
with much more awareness.

665
00:50:36,400 --> 00:50:41,160
Resilience is not a state of being,
but rather a continuous process.

666
00:50:42,560 --> 00:50:46,360
Our psyche is a complex mix
of environmental influences,

667
00:50:46,360 --> 00:50:49,480
genes and our
own ability to act.

668
00:50:51,160 --> 00:50:53,280
Resilience is not
about happiness

669
00:50:53,600 --> 00:50:56,600
it’s about living with
all of life’s gray areas,

670
00:50:56,680 --> 00:50:59,880
surviving crises without
losing your mental health.

671
00:51:00,280 --> 00:51:02,920
To have a realistic
understanding.

672
00:51:08,640 --> 00:51:11,960
Everyone told me it’s
not even worth trying.

673
00:51:11,960 --> 00:51:15,680
I tried anyway, but that doesn’t
mean everything went like I wanted.

674
00:51:15,680 --> 00:51:20,680
Like everyone else, my resilience
is never perfect, never 100%.

675
00:51:21,160 --> 00:51:24,040
I managed to achieve a
lot in spite of everything,

676
00:51:24,040 --> 00:51:27,720
but I haven’t solved everything,
and I think that can be said of you,

677
00:51:27,760 --> 00:51:30,640
of me, of everyone.

